  "identifiers": {
    "part_number": "MRD77400",
    "product": "CFI Apo Lambda S 40XC WI",
    "nominal_magnification": "40x",
    "immersion": "water"
  }
}

objective_change_invalidates_trap_calibr.text:
{
  "entry_id": "objective_change_invalidates_trap_calibration",
  "schema_version": "0.1",
  "kind": "claim",
  "claim": "Changing the objective silently invalidates both of the optical tweezers' calibrations -- the GUI's pixel-to-micrometre magnification and the AOD's trapping-field response -- and neither is readable over TCP. Afterwards every commanded trap position in micrometres lands somewhere else and nothing reports it.",
  "subject": [
    {
      "kind": "device",
      "id": "optical_tweezers"
    },
    {
      "kind": "device",
      "id": "nosepiece"
    },
    {
      "kind": "configuration",
      "id": "trapping"
    }
  ],
  "source": "prior_run:agentic-microscope@safety-2026-09-03",
  "grade": "E3",
  "source_ref": "src_prior_safety_memo",
  "validity_conditions": "Ruled TRANSFER under 10.2.1 and passed 10.3 rule 1, so it caps at E3 -- a run taken in another project is not a run taken here. 10.2's hardware-control-path row is open and says in its own rule that it does not wait for envelope/, because wrapping a control path needs no safety limit. Slot: A4 for the configuration change and O1 preflight for the check it forces, with A7 as the axis that owns commanded motion. It is the specific case of tweez300_reports_nothing_back that bites hardest, because the invalidating action is on a different device from the one that goes wrong. The check is to drive a known amplitude and measure it; the store holds no threshold for what counts as agreement, and that is a caller's judgement recorded in its plan.\n\nNOT THE PIXEL-SIZE CALIBRATION, AND THE STORE NOW SAYS SO AT BOTH ENDS. This entry says an objective change invalidates a pixel-to-micrometre calibration, which reads as covering the twelve pixel_size_<mag>_zoom_<zoom> rows and does not. What it covers is the tweezers' GUI, which holds ONE such calibration that the swap falsifies; the pixel-size rows are TWELVE, indexed BY objective, so the same action selects among them. The person ruled that directly on 2026-09-21 -- see objective_change_selects_a_pixel_size_row. Written into this entry as well as that one because a cross-reference that lives at one end is found only by whoever arrives at that end, and a reader who reaches this entry first is exactly the reader who would over-apply it.",
  "date": "2026-09-19",
  "supersedes": null,
  "conflict_with": [],
  "curated_by": "kyuhwan"
}

objective_change_selects_a_pixel_size_ro.text:
{
  "entry_id": "objective_change_selects_a_pixel_size_row",
  "schema_version": "0.1",
  "kind": "claim",
  "claim": "Changing the objective does not end the twelve per-objective pixel-size calibration rows; it selects which of them applies. A caller that swaps to another objective reads the row naming that objective, at the intermediate magnification in use.",
  "subject": [
    {
      "kind": "device",
      "id": "nosepiece"
    },
    {
      "kind": "quantity",
      "id": "pixel_size"
    }
  ],
  "identifiers": {
    "calibration": "cal-pixel-size-20260919"
  },
  "source": "assumed:person_ruling_20260921",
  "grade": "E5",
  "grade_tag": "person_ruling",
  "source_ref": "src_person_ruling_20260921",
  "date": "2026-09-21",
  "curated_by": "seat:librarian-3",
  "valid_until": {
    "date": "2041-09-19",
    "event": "cal-pixel-size-20260919 is superseded by a new pixel-size calibration. The DATE IS NOT CHOSEN, it is inherited: this claim is about that calibration, so it cannot outlive the calibration's own outer bound, which is the operator's stated fifteen years anchored to 2026-09-19. A claim about a measurement that outlived the measurement would assert something about a number nobody is standing behind any more."
  },
  "validity_conditions": "The twelve entries pixel_size_<mag>_zoom_<zoom>, at 1x1 binning, on this instrument.\n\nWHY THIS IS AN ENTRY AND NOT SOMETHING A READER COULD WORK OUT. The structure is visible -- each row carries `nominal_magnification` and `intermediate_magnification` in `identifiers` rather than in `validity`, and the rows say themselves that those are nominal designations, exact by definition. What is NOT visible is whether that structure is a claim about the instrument or a filing convention, and the store already holds a sentence pointing the other way: objective_change_invalidates_trap_calibration says, in so many words, that changing the objective silently invalidates a pixel-to-micrometre calibration. A reader meeting both had no way to tell which applies where. This entry is the person answering that, asked directly on 2026-09-21.\n\nTHE TWO ARE DIFFERENT AND THE DIFFERENCE IS COUNTABLE. The tweezers hold ONE calibration, and an objective change falsifies it -- worse, neither of the tweezers' two calibrations is readable over TCP, so nothing reports the loss. This calibration holds TWELVE rows INDEXED BY objective, so the same physical action moves the caller between rows instead of destroying one. Both statements are true at once and neither generalises to the other's subject.\n\nWHAT THIS DOES NOT SAY. It does not say that nothing ends these rows. A camera change would end all twelve at once -- pixel size at the sample plane is the sensor pitch over the magnification, and camera_sensor_geometry carries the Kinetix22's 6.5 um pitch -- and so would a change in the optics between objective and camera, which the store holds nothing about. Neither was put to the person, so neither is entered and neither is denied; `valid_until` on the twelve therefore still carries a bare date and no event. See src_person_ruling_20260921 for what was asked and what came back. Looked-for-and-absent and nobody-checked are not the same claim (4.3.1), and that distinction holds outside a gap as well as inside one.\n\nGRADE. E5 because the source is a person's ruling, derived from that and not declared (rule 3). The confidence a reader should put on it is higher than E5 usually signals, because the store's own structure corroborates it -- but corroboration is not a source, and inflating the grade to match a feeling is what the derivation rule exists to prevent.\n\nTHE OTHER HALF OF THE SAME RULING IS NOT AN ENTRY, AND THAT IS NOT AN OVERSIGHT. On 2026-09-21 the person also ruled that this calibration will not be repeated. That was filed beside this one for a few minutes and withdrawn: a decision takes no grade (plan.md:1259 @6d587b3 -- P2's four parts attach to a claim about the world), and kb_entry.schema.json requires one, so it cannot be an entry at all. It is recorded in src_person_ruling_20260921 and in src_operator_calibration_20260919's `standing_2026_09_21`. A READER LOOKING FOR IT THROUGH kb_query WILL NOT FIND IT AND SHOULD NOT: the store answers questions about the world, and whether we intend to remeasure is not one."
}

