  "validity_conditions": "HOW IT WAS OBTAINED: The Kinetix 22's 6.5 um sensor pitch (camera_sensor_geometry, E3, the vendor specification) divided by the nominal total magnification 60x x 1x = 60x: 6.5 / 60 = 0.10833. It rests on camera_sensor_geometry (E3) and on the nominal magnification being exact. That second assumption is known to be imperfect: the one measured row, 20x at 1x, gives 0.32373 against a nominal 0.325, a real 20.078x, so a nominal magnification can be off by about 0.4 per cent and this value by the same.\n\nWHY THIS REPLACES pixel_size_60x_zoom_1x. That row said the operator measured this value, graded E2. On 2026-09-24 the person ruled that only 20x at 1x was measured (src_person_ruling_20260924); the other eleven were calculated. A prior-project Micro-Manager configuration had already said so (recorded on src_operator_calibration_20260919 under contradicted_2026_09_24), and the numbers agree with it: every 1.5x row is its 1x row over 1.5, and five 1x rows are exactly 6.5 um over the nominal magnification. pixel_size_60x_zoom_1x stays, marked refuted_by this entry; its value is not wrong, its claim that it was measured is. `inputs` names `pixel_size` because both operands carry that name -- the sensor pitch in camera_sensor_geometry and the measured row -- and check 62 therefore reports the name divergent rather than composing it; the grade is E4 either way, max(E4, E3) or max(E4, E2).\n\nConditions otherwise as the replaced row: this instrument, 1x1 binning (in `validity`), objective and zoom as exact identifiers. The camera-replacement event in `valid_until` applies with more force here, since the pitch is the numerator outright.",
  "date": "2026-09-24",
